    "narrative": "The **Austin Waves** lineup for July 17 is anchored by the trio of **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (81.21), **Gunnar Henderson** (80.37), and **Jackson Merrill** (75.27). **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** benefits from a favorable matchup against a left-handed starter, receiving a +7% boost from his dynamic platoon profile, while **Gunnar Henderson** remains a top play despite a marginal park factor, fueled by a high-upside platoon split against a righty. **Jackson Merrill** rounds out our elite tier, as his ability to manipulate the zone against an average pitcher makes him a core contributor to our 5x5 totals today. Meanwhile, **Drake Baldwin** (70.50) is a high-reward start, capitalizing on a weak 4.93 xERA opposing pitcher; however, managers must monitor the 30% rain risk in his venue, which could dampen his efficiency if a delay occurs.\n\nPlatoon advantages and lineup placement define our mid-tier production. **Dominic Smith** (60.16) and **Andy Pages** (60.43) both receive boosts from solid park factors and strategic lineup slots, effectively clearing the 60-point threshold. We are taking a cautious approach with **Nasim Nunez** (48.45), whose value is constrained by a bottom-of-the-order assignment and a difficult defensive matchup against an elite catcher and a starter effective at holding base runners. The most difficult bench decision involved **Miguel Vargas**, whose 42.92 score was hampered by a difficult matchup against an elite 3.33 xERA arm; despite his elite 0.401 xwOBA, he couldn't surpass the efficiency of **Curtis Mead** (47.16) or **Sam Antonacci** (50.65), as the latter's leadoff role provides a superior pathway to the Runs category we desperately need. **Heliot Ramos** remains benched, falling below our mandatory 40-point efficiency floor due to a combination of a tough park and an elite-level opposing pitcher, reinforcing our commitment to maximizing every game cap for the stretch run.",
